{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"economic impact\" \"vietnam war\" (+ inflation deficit \"tax surcharge\" \"guns and butter\"))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_790233410#6_1705832934","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1201","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_790233410#6_1705832934\" , \"end_char\": 5062.000000 , \"headings\": \"War and Inflation War and Inflation The Fiscal and Monetary Effects of War The Investment Environment Today \" , \"segment\": \"Professor Fischer also provides an interesting perspective on war-related inflations in the 20th century: \\\"<cover>Inflation surged after America joined World War I in 1917, then declined after 1919, but to pre-war levels. After World War II, Korea and Vietnam, war-inflations were not followed by a decline at all. Prices continued to climb.\" Professor Fischer had some interesting things to say about the Korean War: \"In its economic impact </cover>the Korean War was similar to the world wars that had preceded it. Once again, inflationary pressures surged throughout the world. In 1950, wholesale prices jumped 12% in the United States, 18% in Germany, 21% in Britain, 28% in France, and 32% in Sweden.\" Professor Fischer discusses the inflation that took place as part of the Vietnam War and as part of the 1973 Yom Kippur War. He says that the Vietnam War was "}}
