{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_1805319497#2_2747618688","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage gives useful quantitative context: an inflation trend beginning in 1965 as Vietnam War spending increased, with inflation rising to 4.2% in 1968 and 5.5% in 1969 while unemployment stayed low. However, it is not dedicated to the Vietnam War's economic devastation in 1968 and does not fully explain the war's fiscal damage.","learned":"Inflation rose from 1.6% in 1965, when Vietnam War spending increased, to 4.2% in 1968 and 5.5% in 1969, showing worsening inflation during the war escalation."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vietnam war\" \"galloping inflation\" economy (+ \"budget deficits\" \"weaker dollar\" \"interest rates\"))","reason":"Search for the distinctive economic-damage phrasing from a strong cost source to find additional dedicated documents linking Vietnam War costs to deficits, dollar weakness, inflation, and interest rates."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vietnam war\" \"galloping inflation\" economy (+ \"budget deficits\" \"weaker dollar\" \"interest rates\"))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_30_346273097#3_805100008","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1225","rank":2,"snippet":"Vietnam War The United Kingdom Involvement in the … The Vietnam War Did the U.S lose the Vietnam War? What were major Battles of the … Was American involvement in the … \" , \"segment\": \"During the Vietnam war, in order to meet its required war efforts, factories in the U.S. which used to produce consumer goods were now converted to produce military equipment. This change caused a plunge in shopping rates, thus hurting the economy. Military fund spent overseas also led to budget deficits which caused a weaker dollar, <cover>galloping inflation and increasing interest rates. Owing to the Vietnam War, American economy </cover>was brought down from its growth in early 1960s to an economic crisis in 1970s. Political and Social Cost The United States paid a high political cost for the Vietnam War. Large-scale anti-war movements around the country caused a great rift within American society. Likewise, the leak of Pentagon Papers and the Watergate scandal critically damaged the government credibility. Americans people now kept a deep suspicion and distrust of their government. The failure in Vietnam not only questioned the U.S. superiority as the world defender of freedom and justice but also ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_30_346273097#2_805097986","rank":1,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"23/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_30_346273097#3_805100008","relevance":3,"reason":"This segment is dedicated to Vietnam War costs/economic effects and directly supports severe economic damage: U.S. factories shifted from consumer goods to military production, shopping fell, overseas military spending caused budget deficits, a weaker dollar, galloping inflation, and rising interest rates, bringing the American economy from early-1960s growth toward 1970s crisis.","learned":"Vietnam War spending hurt the U.S. economy through reduced consumer production/shopping, budget deficits, dollar weakness, galloping inflation, higher interest rates, and a shift from early-1960s growth toward 1970s crisis."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vietnam war\" \"war on poverty\" inflation economy (+ \"taxes\" \"johnson\" \"deficit\"))","reason":"Search for Johnson-era 'War on Poverty' plus Vietnam spending evidence, a common explanation for the 1968 economic strain from financing both domestic programs and war without sufficient taxes."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vietnam war\" \"war on poverty\" inflation economy (+ \"taxes\" \"johnson\" \"deficit\"))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_317254117#28_726863651","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1226","rank":3,"snippet":"Timeline Costs Effects Article Sources \" , \"segment\": \"The VA also recently included heart disease among the Vietnam medical issues that qualify. 28 ﻿ Since it is the nation's leading cause of death, it will further increase compensation costs in the years to come. Effects U.S. gross domestic product by year reveals that the war boosted the <cover>economy out of a recession caused by the end of the Korean War in 1953. Spending on the Vietnam War played a small part in causing the Great Inflation that began in 1965. 29 ﻿ But so did spending on the War on Poverty and other LBJ social programs. In 1964, Congress approved a tax cut. The top marginal rate fell from 91% to 70%. That boosted economic growth enough to reduce the level of deficit </cover>spending. In 1965, Johnson signed the law creating Medicare. It helped create a heavy reliance on hospital care, causing rising health c\" , \"start_char\": 9177.000000 , \"title\": \"Vietnam War Facts, Definition, Costs and Timeline\" , \"url\": \"https://www.thebalance.com/vietnam-war-facts-definition-costs-and-","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_30_346287628#0_805117561","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_30_346287628#1_805120016","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
