{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_317254117#28_726863651","relevance":3,"reason":"This is from a dedicated Vietnam War facts/costs/effects page and directly addresses economic effects: it says Vietnam War spending helped boost GDP but also played a part in causing the Great Inflation that began in 1965, alongside War on Poverty and other LBJ programs. It is direct supporting evidence for assessing the war's economic damage around 1968, though it frames the war as only one contributor.","learned":"A dedicated costs/effects source says Vietnam War spending boosted GDP but contributed to the Great Inflation beginning in 1965, along with LBJ domestic spending and tax policy."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1226","reason":"This query is retrieving dedicated sources and segments about Vietnam War spending, LBJ programs, taxes, inflation, and economic effects; continue for more direct evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1226"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_42_1189381891#0_2682667187","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1226","rank":4,"snippet":"flourished for over a decade and had begun to wane. Under conservative Eisenhower, the nation had grown, but only cautiously. When Kennedy swept into office, his energy and enthusiasm inspired Americans to take on challenges both foreign and domestic. Kennedy's goals were to stimulate the economy, reduce unemployment, support growth and democracy abroad and establish an important economic position on the international front. Later, <cover>Johnson added the goals of eradicating poverty, integrating women and minorities into the economy and winning the war in Vietnam. Environmental and consumer interests were increasingly taken into consideration. As the nation strove to achieve these goals, the economy suffered from their negative effects. Large-scale government spending and the constraints of the international monetary system resulted in domestic inflation. As the government struggled to slow inflation and stabilize the economy, the Vietnam War and the War on Poverty </cover>raged on. One war was hopelessly lost, and the other was only partially won.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Overview of the Economy of the 60's\" , \"url\": \"https://www.historycentral.com/sixty/Economics/Overview.html\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"25/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_42_1189381891#0_2682667187","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly connects Johnson's simultaneous goals of winning Vietnam and fighting poverty to economic suffering in the 1960s, saying large-scale government spending and international monetary constraints produced domestic inflation. However, it is an overview of the 1960s economy rather than a document dedicated to how the Vietnam War devastated the economy in 1968.","learned":"Johnson-era spending on Vietnam and the War on Poverty contributed to domestic inflation and economic stabilization problems in the late 1960s."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1226","reason":"This query is still producing useful Johnson/Vietnam/War on Poverty/inflation evidence; continue to look for more dedicated or direct grade-3 results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1226"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_485616401#3_1107107037","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1226","rank":5,"snippet":", \"headings\": \"The U.S. Economy of the1960s and 1970s The U.S. Economy of the1960s and 1970s The 1960s' Effect on the Economy The 1970s' Effect on the Economy \" , \"segment\": \"Ironically, spending on both wars -- the <cover>war on poverty and fighting the war in Vietnam -- contributed to prosperity in the short term. But by the end of the 1960s, the government's failure to raise taxes to pay for these efforts led to accelerating inflation, which eroded this prosperity. The 1970s' Effect on the Economy The 1973-1974 oil embargo by members of the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) pushed energy prices rapidly higher and created shortages. Even after the embargo ended, energy prices stayed high, adding to inflation and eventually causing rising rates of unemployment. Federal budget deficits grew, foreign competition intensified, and the stock market sagged. The Vietnam War </cover>dragged on until 1975, President Richard Nixon (1969-1973) resigned under a cloud of impeachment charges, and a group of Americans were taken hostage at the U.S. embassy in Tehran and held for more than a year. The nation seemed unable to control events, including economic "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"26/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_485616401#3_1107107037","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage gives direct supporting evidence for the late-1960s economic impact: spending on the War on Poverty and Vietnam produced short-term prosperity, but failure to raise taxes by the end of the 1960s accelerated inflation and eroded that prosperity. However, the document is about the U.S. economy of the 1960s and 1970s broadly, not dedicated specifically to the Vietnam War's economic devastation in 1968.","learned":"Vietnam and War on Poverty spending helped prosperity briefly, but failure to raise taxes by the late 1960s caused accelerating inflation that eroded prosperity."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1226","reason":"The current query is still surfacing relevant late-1960s Vietnam/War on Poverty/inflation evidence; continue to seek more direct or dedicated grade-3 results."}}
