{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_2090787545#11_3161794561","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly answers the 1968 economic-impact question: Vietnam escalation fueled inflation and food prices, Johnson sought a 1968 tax increase, the surcharge slowed the U.S. economy, war spending hurt the U.S. balance of trade, and this caused an international monetary crisis and threat to U.S. gold reserves in 1967-68.","learned":"By 1968, Vietnam War escalation had fueled inflation and food prices, forced a tax surcharge that slowed the economy, worsened the balance of trade, and contributed to a 1967-68 international monetary/gold-reserve crisis."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1233","reason":"This query produced a strong 1968-specific economic-impact result; continue to find more direct evidence on inflation, tax increases, deficits, recession, and U.S. economic strain tied to the Vietnam War in 1968."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1233"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_743089204#1_1589784149","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1233","rank":2,"snippet":"the infamous 1929 stock market crash and probably remembers the housing crash and financial crisis of 2008. But many other stock market crashes have rocked the <cover>U.S. economy. Do your homework by learning about the biggest market meltdowns of the past half-century if you want to become a great investor. 1/16 Everett Historical / Shutterstock.com 1. 1969-1970 Stock Market Crash By the 1960s, the economic boom the U.S. had enjoyed since the end of World War II was showing signs of weakness. The stock market endured a steep downturn in 1966, but the market crash of 1969-1970 resulted in an even sharper decline. The causes of the market downturn include the cost of waging the Vietnam War. Stricter monetary policy and rising inflation also contributed to the crash. 2/16 US National Archives and Records / Wikimedia Commons Aftermath of the 1969-1970 Crisis The impact on the stock market was significant: The Standard & Poor's 500 index lost 36.1 percent of its value from November 1968 </cover>to May 1970.\" , \"start_char\": 766.000000 , \"title\": \"Top Market Meltdowns Over the Past 50 "}}
