{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_743089204#1_1589784149","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage gives useful direct support that the cost of the Vietnam War, rising inflation, and tighter monetary policy contributed to the 1969-1970 market crash, with the S&P 500 losing 36.1% from November 1968 to May 1970. However, it is a general stock-market-meltdown article, not dedicated to Vietnam War economic devastation in 1968.","learned":"Vietnam War costs were one cause of the market downturn that began in late 1968; the S&P 500 lost 36.1% from November 1968 to May 1970."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1233","reason":"The 1968/U.S. economy query is producing direct evidence on Vietnam-related inflation, tax increases, balance-of-trade problems, and market decline; continue for more strong or dedicated results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1233"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_03_590161935#7_1022705657","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1233","rank":3,"snippet":"<cover>U.S. Economy Since 1929 Key Takeaways Types of GDP Nominal GDP Real GDP U.S. GDP by Year Since 1929 Compared to Major Events More Economic History Article Sources \" , \"segment\": \"Generally, the year selected as the reference year is the latest year that will not be revised until the next comprehensive update. For the 2018 comprehensive update, real estimates were rebased from chained (2005) dollars to chained (2012) dollars. U.S. GDP by Year Since 1929 Compared to Major Events The following chart tracks both nominal and real GDP since 1929. 3 ﻿ 4 ﻿ 5 ﻿ Some historical events and government policies are added for context. U.S. GDP Year Nominal GDP (trillions) Real GDP (trillions) GDP Growth Rate Events Affecting GDP 1929 $0.105 $1.109 NA Depression began 1930 $0.092 $1.015 -8.5% Smoot-Hawley 1931 $0.077 $0.950 -6.4% Dust Bowl 1932 $0.060 $0.828 -12.9% Hoover tax hikes 1933 $0.057 $0.817 -1.2% New Deal 1934 $0.067 $0.906 10.8% U.S. debt rose 1935 $0.074 $0.986 8.9% Social Security 1936 $0.085 $1.113 12.9% FDR </cover>"}}
