{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_2090787545#10_3161793169","relevance":3,"reason":"This passage directly supports the 1968 economic-impact question: it says Johnson tried to pay for both Vietnam and domestic programs without a tax increase, that the last truly good economic years were 1962-65, that Vietnam escalation fueled inflation and food prices, and that Johnson sought a tax surcharge in 1968 tied to spending cuts.","learned":"Vietnam escalation ended the low-inflation, favorable-balance conditions of 1962-65 by fueling inflation and food prices, forcing Johnson to seek a 1968 tax surcharge while paying for both war and domestic programs."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1234","reason":"This query is producing highly direct 1968 'guns and butter' evidence on Vietnam, inflation, food prices, and tax surcharge; continue to find more direct supporting passages."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1234"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_576260739#9_1027530000","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1234","rank":3,"snippet":"Act of 1968 banned housing discrimination and extended constitutional protections to Native Americans on reservations. During the Johnson presidency the average annual growth rate for the <cover>economy was 4.1 percent. Unemployment during both the Kennedy and Johnson administrations hovered around 4 percent. And by the  end of the Johnson presidency the National Debt was down to around 40 percent from the 60.4 percent that President Kennedy had inherited. Nixon-Ford and Inflation   In the November 1968 election, Eisenhower's vice president,  Richard Nixon, defeated an anti-war candidate, George McGovern, and the Republicans again gained a few seats in the House and Senate. During the Johnson administration a problem with inflation had been brewing – to be inherited, like the war in Vietnam, by President Nixon when he took office in January. High Inflation had accompanied the American Revolutionary War, the Civil War, World War II, the Korean War and now the war in Vietnam. In its war in Vietnam the US had been following a policy of both guns and butter. </cover>Between November 1968 and May 1970 the Dow-Jones Industrial Average plunged  36 percent.\" , \"start_char\": 5438.000000 , \"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_2090787545#11_3161794561","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
