{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_576260739#9_1027530000","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage gives useful direct support: by the end of Johnson's presidency inflation was brewing, high inflation accompanied the Vietnam War under a 'guns and butter' policy, and the Dow fell 36% from November 1968 to May 1970. However, it is a broader political/economic history passage rather than dedicated to how badly the Vietnam War devastated the economy in 1968.","learned":"Vietnam-era 'guns and butter' policy was associated with inflation inherited by Nixon and a 36% Dow decline beginning in November 1968."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1234","reason":"The 'guns and butter' 1968 query is still yielding direct evidence on Vietnam-related inflation, tax surcharge, and market decline; continue to seek additional grade-3 or strong supporting passages."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1234"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_576260739#10_1027531424","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1234","rank":4,"snippet":"inflation had been brewing – to be inherited, like the war in Vietnam, by President Nixon when he took office in January. High <cover>Inflation had accompanied the American Revolutionary War, the Civil War, World War II, the Korean War and now the war in Vietnam. In its war in Vietnam the US had been following a policy of both guns and butter. Between November 1968 and May 1970 the Dow-Jones Industrial Average plunged  36 percent. By the end of  1970 unemployment was up to 6.6 percent. President Nixon believed in the same fiscal responsibility that Eisenhower had pursued during his administration. He supported the government's social programs left by the Roosevelt and Johnson administrations but  wanted them managed better. During the Nixon administration there was an increase in the printing of dollars, and dollars were being sent overseas  at a higher than usual rate to pay for the effort at preventing what was seen as a Communist takeover in Vietnam. What Americans had been unwilling to pay  to the government in the form of higher taxes, </cover>they were now paying   in the form a declining dollar.\" , \"start_char\": 5965.000000 ,"}}
